<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#messapic-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Messapic language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Messapic was an extinct Indo-European language of the Messapians of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salento">Salento</a> peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. It is known from several hundred inscriptions written in an alphabet adapted from the Greek alphabet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>, dating mainly from the sixth to the first centuries BCE. It is not closely related to the other languages of ancient Italy, and some scholars link it with Illyrian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/messapic-language.jpg" alt="Messapic language" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Landon · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messapic_funerary_inscription_(Lecce,_Museo_Castromediano_inv._3646;_MLM_19_Rud).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
